WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.220
Authorization must be tested because the rules change as the application evolves.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:23.000
Useful tests include unit tests for policy functions, integration tests for routes and APIs, negative tests for denied actions, role matrix tests, tenant boundary tests, object-level access tests, service-to-service authorization tests, and regression tests after policy changes.

3
00:00:23.100 --> 00:00:25.420
Happy-path tests are not enough.

4
00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:29.120
The important question is often whether the wrong subject is denied.

5
00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:31.960
Code review should ask direct questions.

6
00:00:32.060 --> 00:00:33.980
Where is authorization enforced?

7
00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:35.520
Is the check server-side?

8
00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:38.740
Does it verify the action and the specific resource?

9
00:00:38.840 --> 00:00:46.440
Does it apply to list endpoints, exports, bulk actions, background jobs, and administrative paths?

10
00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:50.620
Are tenant boundaries enforced in queries as well as object views?

11
00:00:50.720 --> 00:00:53.440
Are hidden buttons being mistaken for security?

12
00:00:53.540 --> 00:00:58.020
Are scopes, claims, and roles interpreted from trusted sources?

13
00:00:58.300 --> 00:01:01.420
Logging supports accountability and incident response.

14
00:01:01.520 --> 00:01:15.600
Access-denied events, privileged actions, permission changes, role assignments, tenant administration, administrative overrides, unusual service account access, and sensitive exports may all be worth recording.

15
00:01:15.700 --> 00:01:24.500
Logs should be privacy-aware and should not expose secrets, tokens, regulated data, or unnecessary sensitive content.

16
00:01:24.600 --> 00:01:31.160
Good logs help teams understand whether policy is working and whether suspicious activity needs investigation.

17
00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:35.360
Monitoring and review keep authorization healthy over time.

18
00:01:35.460 --> 00:01:47.060
Unusual access patterns, repeated denied requests, sudden permission changes, unexpected cross-tenant attempts, and privileged activity outside normal workflows may require triage.

19
00:01:47.160 --> 00:01:49.480
Access reviews help remove stale privilege.

20
00:01:49.580 --> 00:01:53.660
Authorization is not finished when the first check is written.

21
00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:59.760
It remains an engineering, operational, and governance practice throughout the life of the application.
